---
// Same code in production and simulation (#417 (technique pages, part 1)):
// the lines of the register's server, what each one does with the Tokio
// providers, and what it did in this simulated run. The recorded run is
// src/runs/same-code.json; public/same-code.js counts the events of each
// line.
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import Technique from "../../../layouts/Technique.astro";
import { url } from "../../../nav";
import { constStr, excerpt, hintLabels, lineWith, repoFile } from "../../../lib/source.mjs";
import runs from "../../../runs/same-code.json";

const register = repoFile("crates/moonpool-wasm-demo/src/register.rs");
const struck = constStr(repoFile("crates/moonpool-wasm-demo/src/engine.rs"), "HINT_STRUCK");
const [beforeSync, beforeAck] = hintLabels(register);
const ROWS = [
  { line: lineWith(register, ".open(FILE,"), prod: "TokioStorageProvider opens a file on the real disk.", event: "server_boot" },
  { line: lineWith(register, "listener.accept()"), prod: "TokioNetworkProvider accepts a TCP connection.", event: "msg_recv", role: "process" },
  { line: lineWith(register, ".write_at("), prod: "The kernel copies the record to its page cache.", event: "log_written" },
  { line: lineWith(register, `hint!("${beforeSync}")`), prod: "Resolves at once. It draws nothing and asks no one.", event: struck, label: beforeSync },
  { line: lineWith(register, "file.sync_all()"), prod: "fsync: the record is on the disk.", event: "log_synced" },
  { line: lineWith(register, `hint!("${beforeAck}")`), prod: "Resolves at once.", event: struck, label: beforeAck },
  { line: lineWith(register, "if buggify!()"), prod: "Always false. No draw.", event: "reply_dropped" },
  { line: lineWith(register, "stream.write_all(&reply.encode())"), prod: "TokioNetworkProvider sends the reply.", event: "msg_send", role: "process" },
];
const boot = excerpt(register, "async fn run(&mut self, ctx: &SimContext)", "let listener = ctx.network().bind(ctx.my_ip()).await?;");
---
<Technique
  title="Same code in production and simulation"
  description="One server, two sets of providers. The simulator runs the code you ship."
  guide={[
    "part2-foundations/12-same-code.md",
    "part2-foundations/04-provider-pattern.md",
    "part3-building/08-buggify.md",
    "part3-building/29-hints.md",
  ]}
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>A simulator that tests a model of your system finds the bugs of the model. When the test runs a copy of
    the code, or the code with test-only hooks, the copy and the shipped code go apart. After some time, the
    test tests itself.</p>

  <h2>The technique</h2>
  <p>The code does all its I/O through <b>providers</b>: the time, the tasks, the network, the disk and the
    random numbers. In production, the Tokio providers do real I/O. In a simulation, the simulated providers
    do it on one thread, and a seed decides each delay and each fault. The code above the providers is the
    same compiled code.</p>
  <p>Two marks let the code help the simulator. <code>buggify!()</code> is a rare choice that is legal, for
    example "drop this reply". <code>hint!("…")</code> names a moment where a reboot hurts most, and attrition
    can reboot the process there. In production both are inert: <code>buggify!()</code> is false and <code>hint!</code> resolves at once.</p>

  <h2>The live run</h2>
  <p>Each row is one line of the register's server. The middle column is what it does in production. The
    right column counts what it did in this run, up to the cursor. At 364 ms the simulator reboots
    10.0.1.1 at its first hint, between the write and the sync. Select a hint and find the next seed that
    strikes there.</p>
  <div class="breakout">
    <section class="run" id="run" aria-label="A live run"></section>
    <div class="run run-under">
      <div class="run-head label"><span>the server's code, line by line</span></div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="run-table" id="code-table">
          <tr><th>line of register.rs</th><th>in production</th><th>in this simulation</th></tr>
          {ROWS.map((r) => (
            <tr data-event={r.event} data-label={r.label} data-role={r.role}>
              <td><code>{r.line}</code></td>
              <td>{r.prod}</td>
              <td class="sim later">not yet</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </table>
      </div>
      <form class="switches" id="strike-form">
        <label for="strike-label" class="label">hint</label>
        <select id="strike-label" name="label">
          {[beforeSync, beforeAck].map((l) => <option value={l}>{l}</option>)}
        </select>
        <button class="btn small" type="submit">Find the next seed that strikes</button>
      </form>
      <p class="run-out" id="strike-out" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </div>
    <noscript><p>The live run needs JavaScript.</p></noscript>
  </div>
  <script is:inline type="application/json" id="runs" set:html={JSON.stringify(runs).replace(/</g, "\\u003c")} />
  <script is:inline type="application/json" id="same-code-data" set:html={JSON.stringify({ struck, tries: 200 })} />

  <h2>The code</h2>
  <p>The server gets its providers from its context. It does not know if they are real or simulated:</p>
  <Code theme="css-variables" lang="rust" code={boot} />
  <p>The register runs only in the simulator, so it gets its providers from <code>SimContext</code>. A library
    that ships takes <code>P: Providers</code> and gets <code>TokioProviders</code> in production. The
    Guide chapter shows how a crate keeps the simulator out of its release build.</p>
  <script is:inline type="module" src={url("same-code.js")}></script>
</Technique>
